\section{Introduction}
Understanding the emergence of ordered phases in many-body systems is often facilitated by the presence of symmetry. In this paper, we construct a system comprising agents following two distinct dynamical rules: those governed by the Ising model and those updating via voter model dynamics. Our simulations reveal that even a trace amount of Ising agents is sufficient to induce long-range ordering in the otherwise fully demagnetized voter model system. This observation prompts the question of whether this effect is specific to these two models or reflects a broader phenomenon arising from the mixture of two systems with different dynamical symmetries. This curiosity drives our theoretical investigation, which uncovers a mechanism rooted in the lack of phase slip paths in the energy landscape of the majority voter model system—a property responsible for the absence of true phase transitions in infinite systems. This mechanism also accounts for the dependence of susceptibility on the concentration of Ising agents. Ultimately, we propose that these phenomena represent a form of "quasi-ergodicity," distinguishing it from standard ergodicity. This work serves as a foundational step toward studying opinion formation in systems with more complex dynamical symmetries.
